## 4. Applications of MOF-based ECL systems
ECL-active MOFs have been widely applied in biosensors due to their distinct characteristics in selective capture of analytes (Hu et al., 2014; Liao et al., 2019). Here, recent advances in MOF-based analysis are described with a focus on immunoassay, ECL genosensors, and small molecule detection.
## 4.1 ECL Immunoassay
ECL immunoassay has gained much interest in biomedical applications due to the integration of the specific immunoreactions with the intrinsic properties of ECL. Cancers have long been the major health threat worldwide, and the cancer markers, such as NT-proBNP, Aβ, CEA, and PSA, associated with certain tumors play important roles in diseases diagnosis, implying the necessity to develop ultrasensitive detection methods for early and sensitive determination of disease markers. For instance, increased concentration of NT-proBNP is considered as a biomarker for diagnosis of heart failure (Richards and Troughton, 2004). Biocompatible $\mathrm{Ce-MOF@g-C_{3}N_{4}/A u}$ nanocomposites were used as signal-amplified ECL probes for label-free NT-proBNP immunoassay (Li et al., 2019). The porous matrix of Ce-MOF not only amplified the ECL signal of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets, but also obviously improved their interfacial stability. In another work, Xiong et al. designed a signal amplified ECL sensor for sensitive NT-proBNP measurement by employing $[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ doped MOFs as ECL nano-labels due to the excellent accumulation capability of MOFs towards $[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ (Xiong et al., 2015). $\mathbf{A}\boldsymbol{\upbeta}$ has been recognized as one of the reliable indicators for diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease, implicating the importance
of sensitive detection of $\mathbf{A}\beta$ for early assessment of Alzheimer's disease. Cao’s group fabricated a sensitive immunosensor for $\mathbf{A}\beta$ determination by using $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO-66 and MIL-101, which can increase the immobilization amount of antibodies due to excellent porosity and abundant functional groups within these dual MOFs (Dong et al., 2020). In another report, an ultrasensitive immunosensor was fabricated for $\mathbf{A}\beta$ determination via ECL-RET between Au nanoparticles-loaded $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets and Pd NPs coated $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 for signal amplification (Fang et al., 2019a). Au NPs not only enhanced the ECL signal of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , but also increased the biocompatibility of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ for antibody modification. A notable diminishing ECL signal can be observed when incubating Pd $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 onto the immunosensor because Pd $\mathsf{N P s@N H_{2}}$ -MIL-53(Al) can quench the ECL signal of g-C3N4@Au NPs (Fig.6), enabling ultrasensitive and selective determination of Aβ.   
Since its first report by Yuan's group in 2014 (Zhuo et al., 2014), the signal amplification strategy of self-enhanced ECL complex has been successfully applied in ECL immunosensors. For example, an intramolecular self-enhanced ECL immunosensor was proposed for procalcitonin quantification due to the existence of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ and its co-reactant (PEI) in the same complex (Wang et al., 2019b). More importantly, the leakage of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ was hampered tactfully because of PEI decorated on the surface of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ doped MOFs. The $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ -PEI enriched MIL-101(Al)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF with high emission efficiency was used to establish a “signal off” ECL immunosensing platform. In another report, on the basis of proximity-induced intramolecular DNA strand displacement, PCN-777 was used as an excellent nanocarrier of $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{2}(\mathrm{mcpbpy)}^{2+}$ luminophore and co-reactant PEI to synthesize self-enhanced ECL complex for MUC1 immunosensing (Hu et al., 2018). All the above self-enhanced ECL immunosensors achieved successful target detection. A summary of different MOF composites for ECL immunoassay is listed in Table 3.   
